1
00:00:00,163 --> 00:00:04,303
It's eight a.m. You haven't touched
your laptop since last night.

2
00:00:04,411 --> 00:00:05,711
But your bot has been busy.

3
00:00:05,731 --> 00:00:07,551
Nine pull requests ready for review.

4
00:00:07,811 --> 00:00:09,131
Four already merged.

5
00:00:09,313 --> 00:00:11,073
And one that needs a human.

6
00:00:11,093 --> 00:00:12,653
That's you.

7
00:00:13,605 --> 00:00:14,625
This isn't a fantasy.

8
00:00:14,645 --> 00:00:18,905
In xAI's own engineering guide, one
engineer describes managing fifteen

9
00:00:19,165 --> 00:00:22,085
cloud coding agents by hand.

10
00:00:22,308 --> 00:00:27,048
Now their bots manage more than two
hundred of them at the same time.

11
00:00:27,943 --> 00:00:29,463
The difference isn't a better prompt.

12
00:00:29,483 --> 00:00:31,770
It's the job you give Grok Bot.

13
00:00:32,430 --> 00:00:33,870
Most people make it the coder.

14
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:37,150
The people shipping the
most make it the manager.

15
00:00:38,207 --> 00:00:42,687
This video is the playbook: how to
set it up, how to hand off work, and

16
00:00:42,707 --> 00:00:45,347
how to keep the loop
closed while you sleep.

17
00:00:46,229 --> 00:00:50,329
Here's the trap. Grok Bot has a terminal
and a browser on a cloud computer,

18
00:00:50,649 --> 00:00:52,209
so yes, it can write code.

19
00:00:52,418 --> 00:00:56,098
But every bot shares that one machine
and its logins, and you'd be reviewing

20
00:00:56,118 --> 00:00:58,878
a chat transcript instead of a diff.

21
00:00:59,203 --> 00:01:01,103
So give it the job it's built for.

22
00:01:01,123 --> 00:01:04,943
It hands the code to a cloud agent,
demands proof, and watches the pull

23
00:01:04,963 --> 00:01:06,603
request until it's green.

24
00:01:07,425 --> 00:01:08,625
Think of it as two loops.

25
00:01:08,665 --> 00:01:12,565
The inner loop is a coding agent:
one repo, one task, real tests.

26
00:01:13,044 --> 00:01:14,604
The outer loop is Grok Bot.

27
00:01:14,624 --> 00:01:19,204
It reads Slack, the bug tracker and
your messages, and turns them into that

28
00:01:19,224 --> 00:01:19,844
one task.

29
00:01:20,170 --> 00:01:22,570
The brainstorming and the
dead ends stay outside.

30
00:01:22,590 --> 00:01:25,710
The agent only ever sees a clean brief.

31
00:01:26,446 --> 00:01:31,086
And on September thirtieth, the Grok
Bot team leaned straight into this.

32
00:01:31,286 --> 00:01:35,746
Bots can now hand off coding tasks to
Cursor, manage pull requests through

33
00:01:35,766 --> 00:01:39,126
GitHub, and share video
demos of what they build.

34
00:01:40,019 --> 00:01:44,239
So split the work. The bot gathers
context and writes the brief.

35
00:01:44,259 --> 00:01:46,619
The agent edits code and runs the tests.

36
00:01:46,947 --> 00:01:48,907
The bot checks the proof
and keeps going overnight.

37
00:01:48,927 --> 00:01:50,767
And merging stays with you,

38
00:01:51,327 --> 00:01:53,527
unless the change is genuinely low risk.

39
00:01:54,348 --> 00:01:58,528
Chapter one. Set up the team
before you hand it any work.

40
00:01:59,347 --> 00:02:03,507
In the official guide, each engineer
bot owns one area: iOS, the desktop

41
00:02:03,527 --> 00:02:07,647
app and its CI, infrastructure,
Android, and the bot harness itself.

42
00:02:07,864 --> 00:02:12,164
They can work across areas, but each
has its own memory and limited context,

43
00:02:12,564 --> 00:02:15,057
so they're sharpest where
they own the specs.

44
00:02:16,036 --> 00:02:19,256
Then write each bot's
role in three layers.

45
00:02:19,646 --> 00:02:20,886
Rules that never change.

46
00:02:20,946 --> 00:02:24,486
The job it owns. And the current
assignment, which you can swap without

47
00:02:24,506 --> 00:02:25,466
touching the rules.

48
00:02:26,268 --> 00:02:27,968
One warning before you connect anything.

49
00:02:27,988 --> 00:02:30,828
Every bot on your account
shares one cloud computer,

50
00:02:31,288 --> 00:02:33,468
with the same files and logins.

51
00:02:33,768 --> 00:02:35,688
Separate bots are not a security boundary.

52
00:02:35,708 --> 00:02:38,048
So give them a fork or a scoped token.

53
00:02:38,608 --> 00:02:39,888
Never deploy keys.

54
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:43,680
Enter secrets through the secure input,
never in chat, and keep them out

55
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:46,420
of slash workspace, where
every bot can read them.

56
00:02:47,152 --> 00:02:49,412
Chapter two. The handoff.

57
00:02:50,545 --> 00:02:51,785
Here's what that looks like.

58
00:02:51,805 --> 00:02:54,145
You drop a one-line bug report.

59
00:02:54,527 --> 00:02:58,927
The bot pulls the Slack thread and
the crash trace, then starts a Cursor

60
00:02:58,947 --> 00:03:01,207
cloud agent with the right skills loaded.

61
00:03:01,357 --> 00:03:04,887
And it says, up front, what
proof it expects back.

62
00:03:05,958 --> 00:03:07,518
That brief is the whole game.

63
00:03:07,538 --> 00:03:10,218
The task. The context it already gathered.

64
00:03:10,450 --> 00:03:11,550
Which skills to load.

65
00:03:11,570 --> 00:03:12,670
What proof to bring back.

66
00:03:12,690 --> 00:03:15,990
And when to stop and ask,
instead of guessing.

67
00:03:17,226 --> 00:03:19,846
That's the outer loop
protecting the inner one.

68
00:03:19,866 --> 00:03:21,706
The messy thinking stays with the bot.

69
00:03:22,086 --> 00:03:23,906
The agent gets a clean task.

70
00:03:24,733 --> 00:03:27,573
Chapter three. Close the loop.

71
00:03:28,356 --> 00:03:32,896
The single most important idea in the
guide: give the agent a complete feedback

72
00:03:32,916 --> 00:03:33,436
loop.

73
00:03:33,577 --> 00:03:35,717
The agent works and attaches proof.

74
00:03:35,737 --> 00:03:39,637
The bot checks it, and if it doesn't
match the request, it pushes back.

75
00:03:39,812 --> 00:03:42,232
Round after round, until the goal is met.

76
00:03:42,632 --> 00:03:45,112
Not until the agent says it's done.

77
00:03:46,067 --> 00:03:47,947
Cloud agents can attach screenshots,

78
00:03:48,627 --> 00:03:51,007
and the bot really looks at them.

79
00:03:51,318 --> 00:03:54,678
Here, the button is still clipped on
the smallest screen, so it goes back

80
00:03:54,698 --> 00:03:56,418
to the agent instead of to you.

81
00:03:56,527 --> 00:04:00,227
Round two. Fixed, tests green, verified.

82
00:04:01,306 --> 00:04:04,766
For that loop to work, the agent has
to drive the stack end to end: launch

83
00:04:04,786 --> 00:04:07,706
a dev build, click through
it, capture the result.

84
00:04:07,899 --> 00:04:09,399
Chrome DevTools for the web.

85
00:04:09,419 --> 00:04:11,219
A CLI for services.

86
00:04:11,739 --> 00:04:14,279
Accessibility APIs for the iOS simulator.

87
00:04:14,719 --> 00:04:19,459
And when it figures out a flow, have
it package that into a reusable repo

88
00:04:19,479 --> 00:04:19,939
skill.

89
00:04:20,782 --> 00:04:22,022
So here's the rule.

90
00:04:22,042 --> 00:04:25,902
If the agent can't check its own
work, it can't work without you.

91
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:30,360
Chapter four. Scale past
the context window.

92
00:04:31,432 --> 00:04:36,772
Long chats scroll away, so the bots
keep a shared database of every pull

93
00:04:36,792 --> 00:04:37,272
request.

94
00:04:37,639 --> 00:04:41,799
Every thirty minutes, they sweep
it: bug-bot comments, failing CI,

95
00:04:42,339 --> 00:04:43,419
merge conflicts.

96
00:04:44,015 --> 00:04:47,495
Anything off goes straight back to
the agent, and the row moves back to

97
00:04:47,515 --> 00:04:47,995
Working.

98
00:04:48,475 --> 00:04:50,375
Clean ones move to Ready for review.

99
00:04:51,255 --> 00:04:53,575
Clean PRs get an automatic code review.

100
00:04:53,595 --> 00:04:57,835
If the review is highly confident and
the blast radius is low, it merges

101
00:04:57,855 --> 00:04:58,515
on its own.

102
00:04:58,629 --> 00:05:02,589
Everything else waits for you, with the
proof attached, so your review takes

103
00:05:02,609 --> 00:05:04,509
minutes instead of an afternoon.

104
00:05:05,425 --> 00:05:07,245
Here's what the team
behind Grok Bot reports.

105
00:05:07,265 --> 00:05:11,685
One engineer shipped more than two
thousand pull requests in a month.

106
00:05:11,730 --> 00:05:14,870
The foundation of Grok Bot
itself was built in four weeks.

107
00:05:14,890 --> 00:05:18,510
The first iOS app, in
three, using only Grok Bot.

108
00:05:19,393 --> 00:05:22,053
Chapter five. The night shift.

109
00:05:23,132 --> 00:05:25,512
Routines turn all of this into a schedule.

110
00:05:25,532 --> 00:05:28,512
Every thirty minutes, the PR sweep.

111
00:05:28,776 --> 00:05:32,796
At three a.m., the nightly audit:
dead code, load time, bundle size.

112
00:05:32,836 --> 00:05:35,676
At five, the ops bot
meets every engineer bot.

113
00:05:35,741 --> 00:05:41,081
And at eight, you wake up to a list
of PRs that are ready to merge.

114
00:05:41,990 --> 00:05:45,710
The guide lists audits worth stealing.

115
00:05:45,896 --> 00:05:47,616
Security sweeps. CI build time.

116
00:05:47,636 --> 00:05:49,216
Missing translations.

117
00:05:49,556 --> 00:05:51,776
Parity between your iOS and desktop apps.

118
00:05:51,796 --> 00:05:55,696
And a catch-up summary of
everything merged in the last day.

119
00:05:56,417 --> 00:05:58,257
And their favorite nightly prompt of all.

120
00:05:58,277 --> 00:05:59,717
You have six hours tonight.

121
00:05:59,737 --> 00:06:01,157
Build whatever you want.

122
00:06:01,377 --> 00:06:02,257
Have fun.

123
00:06:03,162 --> 00:06:06,802
A few details from the
docs will save you money.

124
00:06:07,095 --> 00:06:08,935
Schedules must be at
least five minutes apart.

125
00:06:09,055 --> 00:06:10,295
Pressing Test does real work.

126
00:06:10,315 --> 00:06:13,715
And never use a broad trigger,
like every new message.

127
00:06:13,941 --> 00:06:18,101
If you're away for a long time, routines
can be paused until you respond.

128
00:06:19,087 --> 00:06:24,107
One more trick. When something is
truly urgent, tell the bot it's P0.

129
00:06:24,415 --> 00:06:28,055
It starts a temporary routine that
checks the agent's transcript every five

130
00:06:28,075 --> 00:06:28,595
minutes,

131
00:06:29,215 --> 00:06:31,215
and steers it when it wastes time.

132
00:06:31,326 --> 00:06:35,186
It burns tokens fast, so save
it for real emergencies.

133
00:06:36,522 --> 00:06:39,782
Chapter six. Keep the org sharp.

134
00:06:40,749 --> 00:06:43,229
The one bot that writes
no code is the ops bot.

135
00:06:43,249 --> 00:06:47,609
When an engineer bot makes a mistake,
the ops bot runs a postmortem.

136
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:51,540
It finds the root cause, updates the
playbook, and announces the change

137
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:56,240
to every bot. Then it repeats it in a
daily one-on-one, so nobody forgets.

138
00:06:56,851 --> 00:06:59,491
And when a bot struggles, treat
it like a talented intern.

139
00:06:59,611 --> 00:07:04,391
Ask it to do its homework, and to study
how your best engineers do the job.

140
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:06,080
Now the honest part.

141
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:07,640
Don't use it for deterministic jobs.

142
00:07:07,660 --> 00:07:12,180
If the inputs and rules are fixed,
write a script or a CI job.

143
00:07:12,311 --> 00:07:14,091
Keep it out of hard real-time paths.

144
00:07:14,231 --> 00:07:16,411
Don't treat separate bots
as separate trust zones.

145
00:07:16,431 --> 00:07:19,161
And if you need to choose
the exact model, there's

146
00:07:19,651 --> 00:07:20,671
no model picker.

147
00:07:21,430 --> 00:07:22,650
Here's how to start this week.

148
00:07:22,670 --> 00:07:25,070
Create one engineer bot for one domain.

149
00:07:25,090 --> 00:07:27,590
Write a handoff skill that demands proof.

150
00:07:27,765 --> 00:07:31,425
Run one real task by hand, and fix
the process, not just the output.

151
00:07:31,445 --> 00:07:34,355
Then add the thirty-minute PR sweep.

152
00:07:34,587 --> 00:07:38,707
Only when that runs clean for a
week, turn on the nightly audit.

153
00:07:39,516 --> 00:07:41,196
Don't ask Grok Bot to write your code.

154
00:07:41,216 --> 00:07:43,336
Ask it to run your loop.

155
00:07:44,057 --> 00:07:48,417
If you want to see this machine run
a whole video studio overnight, that

156
00:07:48,437 --> 00:07:49,017
one's next.
